{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "174",
    "case_summary": "A woman in her 50s with a history of chronic pulmonary coccidiomycosis being treated with fluconazole was admitted for fever, hypotension, and rash. Three months prior, pembrolizumab treatment was stopped because of a widespread morbilliform reaction, which had resolved completely 6 to 8 weeks before admission. The new widespread, asymptomatic skin eruption had been ongoing for 1 to 2 weeks and appeared different from her previous reaction.\n\nTotal body skin examination findings were notable for widespread, nontender, erythematous patches and thin plaques, some with prominent overlying telangiectases and hyperpigmentation.\n\n- Laboratory tests: Complete blood cell count showed white blood cell count of 4100/μL, absolute neutrophil count of 2940/μL, hemoglobin level of 8.2 g/dL, and platelet count of 62 ×103/μL. Blood culture results showed no growth at 1 week. Serum β-D-glucan, galactomannan, Coccidioides antibody by complement fixation, and immunodiffusion results were negative. Respiratory viral panel and SARS-CoV-2 test results were negative.- Pathology:   Image Title: Skin Biopsy Findings  Image Description: Hematoxylin-eosin stained sections and CD56 immunostaining showing intravascular atypical cells.- Additional Tests:  - Brain MRI performed  - Lumbar puncture performed  - Bone marrow biopsy performed",
    "root": {
      "label": "Subacute Widespread Skin Eruption with Systemic Symptoms and Haematological Abnormalities",
